From d21f178f60fccd516a87f1b7cb20417d0422b4c1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: onetr1ck <wilhelm.arngold@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 14:52:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 8/8] tests: Cover Night Light GAMMA_LUT preference and CTM
 fallback

Teach MetaCrtcTest about CTM so the color management tests can describe
a CRTC with CTM but no gamma LUT, and add four cases:

- prefer-gamma-lut: a CRTC exposing both must program the LUT and leave
  the CTM untouched, which is the property the fallback must never break
- ctm-fallback: a CRTC with only CTM gets a diagonal matrix carrying the
  blackbody scales for the temperature, with no color profile assigned
- ctm-scale-clamping: out of range and non-finite scales are clamped, so
  nothing outside [0, 1] can reach the kernel
- supported: NightLightSupported follows CTM capability, and is still
  reported for a gamma-only CRTC

The two hardware cases drive the real path from the gsd-color temperature
down to the CRTC. Each test picks temperatures no other test leaves
behind, since the color manager only acts on a temperature that actually
changed, and the wait for that update is bounded: these tests have no
color profile assigned, so a regression that makes the update bail out
early emits nothing, which would otherwise surface as an unexplained
suite timeout rather than a named failure.
